\begin{abstract}
Multi-judge LLM evaluation panels are assumed to benefit from diversity, yet diversity's role under adversarial manipulation is poorly understood. We decompose panel attack success into individual judge weakness ($\eta_{\max}$) and structural diversity ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) across 15 models, six attack types, two benchmarks, and panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (up to 6,435 panels). Consistent with adversarial ensemble theory but newly characterized in the LLM judge setting, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is uniformly positively associated with vulnerability across all 12 conditions, though the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) limits causal interpretation. The predictors' relative importance shifts with panel size: at $K{=}3$, both contribute; at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the sole significant predictor. Sensitivity analysis shows $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s robustness to hypothetical confounders increases with panel size (RV exceeds the known confound in 1/12 at $K{=}3$ to 4/12 at $K{=}7$; 10/12 at $K{=}5$ in a capability-matched subsample). Aggregation rules modulate predictability ($R^2$: threshold 0.83, Bayesian 0.09). The defense strategy must match the panel size: small panels require screening for individual robustness; larger panels require managing judge independence.
\end{abstract}

\item \textbf{Aggregation as predictability control.} Aggregation rules modulate how much variance panel composition explains ($R^2$: threshold 0.83 $\to$ Bayesian 0.09). Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12), providing partial rather than complete defense (\S\ref{sec:aggregation}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

Our findings describe descriptive associations; causal attribution is limited by the diversity--capability confound (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

The methods form a predictability gradient from threshold ($R^2{=}0.829$) to Bayesian ($R^2{=}0.087$; Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_details}). Threshold voting's high $R^2$ is partly structural: unanimity makes panel failure nearly deterministic given the weakest judge. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s positive direction is preserved across majority (12+/0$-$) and weighted (12+/0$-$) voting; Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) and reduces overall predictability ($R^2{=}0.087$), but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12 under OLS), yielding partial rather than complete defense.

\paragraph{Fractional Logit Robustness.}
Because ASR $\in [0,1]$ is a bounded proportion, we verify the OLS log-log results using fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered standard errors. At $K{=}3$, fractional logit gives $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 9/12 significant, higher than both OLS clustered SE (1/12) and wild bootstrap (4/12). The predictor shift is clearer under fractional logit: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance increases from 9/12 ($K{=}3$) to 11/12 ($K{=}5$) to 12/12 ($K{=}7$), with 98.6\% directional concordance with OLS (71/72; Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}).

Recomputing MI under attack confirms stability ($\rho{\geq}0.91$ for 4/6 attacks; position bias disrupts MI on ARC, $\rho{=}0.08$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}). The random-fault contrast (\S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}) confirms $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ predicts under non-adaptive noise ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$), with the predictability collapse most pronounced at $K{=}3$.

\paragraph{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ vs.\ Alternative Diversity Measures.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is near-equivalent to $\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} ($\rho{=}0.978$) and disagreement rate ($\rho{=}0.934$) in empirical predictive power: $\kappa$-diversity achieves 11/12 significant conditions with $|\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}|{=}0.497$ versus $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s 12/12 and 0.430 (Appendix~\ref{app:alt_diversity}). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s advantage is not empirical prediction but \emph{decomposability}: the SDPI bound $\eta_{\mathrm{joint}} \leq K_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \eta_{\max}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:monotonicity}) separates panel vulnerability into individual weakness and structural diversity, providing two actionable dimensions for panel design. $\kappa$-diversity measures diversity but does not connect to a vulnerability bound; disagreement rate conflates diversity with capability differences.

